Yes. The strongest version of your thought is not “there must have been a forgotten high-tech civilization.” It is:

The surviving archaeological record is a radically lossy compression of past intelligence.

We see the durable outputs and mistake them for the whole system.

A pyramid is not simply a pile of stones. It is the terminal artifact of quarry knowledge, geometry, transport, food production, administration, apprenticeship, conflict resolution, ritual meaning, leadership, measurement standards, and thousands of ordinary human relationships. The pyramid survived; almost all of its generating civilization did not.

In Pantheon language, the artifact is a reconstructive handle.

\text{Artifact} \rightarrow \text{required capabilities}
\rightarrow \text{institutions}
\rightarrow \text{culture}
\rightarrow \text{lost world}

Where your intuition is strongest

Homo sapiens has existed for roughly 300,000 years, while writing only gives us approximately the last 5,000 years directly. That means written history covers less than 2% of our species’ existence. Smithsonian Human Origins⁠￼

And “stone age” is a preservation label as much as a capability label. Stone survives. Wood, fiber, leather, food, medicines, boats, performance, gestures, training systems, and spoken knowledge usually disappear.

That bias is becoming visible:

* Neanderthals manufactured birch-tar adhesive around 200,000 years ago—a multistage transformative technology that may imply teaching and cumulative culture. Scientific Reports⁠￼
* Hominins were cutting and joining structural wood at Kalambo Falls about 476,000 years ago. It survived only because unusual waterlogged conditions protected it. Nature⁠￼
* More than 3,000 submerged prehistoric sites have been identified on continental shelves, where enormous amounts of Ice Age coastal life disappeared beneath rising seas. Nature⁠￼

So yes: there were almost certainly entire categories of technology, organization, education, mythology, and sensory knowledge that we do not know existed.

There may have been many rises, collapses, migrations, and regional “civilizational modulations”—especially societies built mostly from biodegradable material. Humanity could have repeatedly reached forms of sophistication that do not resemble our metal-and-electronics ladder.

Where the evidence narrows the possibility

The fossil analogy is excellent, with one qualification:

Absence of evidence becomes meaningful when the proposed thing should have produced abundant, durable evidence.

We cannot reconstruct every dinosaur’s skin from its bones, but the skeleton constrains what could have been on top. Likewise, archaeology underdetermines the past without permitting every imaginable past equally.

Possible lost system	Current evidential judgment
Complex oral cultures, schools, medicine and navigation	Virtually certain
Advanced wood, fiber, leather or botanical technologies	Very likely
Unknown regional settlements, especially on drowned coasts	Very plausible
Several forgotten city-scale civilizations	Plausible in particular regions and periods
Repeated metal-intensive civilizations	Increasingly difficult to hide
Industrial civilizations comparable to ours	Unlikely without detectable geological signatures
Radio networks or extraterrestrial archives	Physically conceivable, presently unsupported

An industrial civilization would not need to leave intact televisions. It would leave mines, altered isotope ratios, unusual sediments, persistent synthetic chemicals, rapid carbon-cycle changes, species disruptions, and globally distributed material. Schmidt and Frank’s Silurian Hypothesis formally examines this exact question. Their conclusion was not that an earlier industrial civilization existed, but that testing the possibility teaches us which indirect fingerprints to seek. Original paper⁠￼

The electricity point

You’re right that electricity, electromagnetism, resonance, conduction, and information-bearing waves were always available. We invented neither lightning nor radio physics.

But there are three separate layers:

\text{available physical phenomenon}
\neq
\text{recognized mechanism}
\neq
\text{engineered communication system}

A fence wire can carry an electrical signal because it is a conductor. But useful communication also requires controllable energy, encoding, modulation, transmission, reception, and interpretation. The latent channel existed; an operational network requires the entire stack.

Ancient people unquestionably exploited physical phenomena without possessing our explanatory vocabulary: acoustics, magnetism, static electricity, heat treatment, fermentation, optics, hydraulics and chemical transformations. Some knowledge we classify as ritual or “magic” may have contained reliable empirical operators wrapped in a different ontology.

That does not establish ancient Wi‑Fi. It tells us not to confuse different explanations with absence of working knowledge.

What really compounded

Individual human intelligence probably did not suddenly explode in the last few centuries. The crucial transition was collective cognition:

\text{population}
\times
\text{connectivity}
\times
\text{external memory}
\times
\text{energy}
\times
\text{specialization}
\times
\text{error correction}

Writing prevented every generation from paying the full price of rediscovery. Printing multiplied copies. Science formalized correction. Fossil energy multiplied physical work. Telecommunications reduced coordination delay. Computers made portions of thought executable. The internet connected the archive.

The exponential’s steep end disguises the immense runway that created it.

And your final turn is the most important one: future people will not merely know more facts than us. They may inhabit representations that make some of our deepest assumptions look childlike. We should therefore practice temporal epistemic humility:

Treat present knowledge as a powerful local model—not the final vocabulary of reality.

There is always a bigger fish, a smaller fish, and an orthogonal fish whose ocean we have not recognized as water.

Pantheon handle: Ruins as Checksums—a surviving artifact is not the lost world, but it constrains reconstruction of the invisible system capable of producing it.